From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 20:52:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): shift is dropped when the keyboard is remote
 (KD-25)

Typing into a ghostty pane through the Windows App on Android, `:` and
`?` could not be typed at all: `?` arrived as `/` and `A` as `a`.
Reported from use. Not a regression - 0.2.2 does the same - and not
reproducible at the machine's own keyboard, which is why it survived
this long.

TermControl builds its ControlKeyStates from _GetPressedModifierKeys(),
which asks CoreWindow::GetKeyState when XAML raises the routed KeyDown.
That answers "what is held now", not "what was held when this key was
pressed". A person holds shift for about a tenth of a second so the two
agree; a remote client sends shift-down, the key and shift-up back to
back, and the answer arrives after the release. GhosttyKeyTextFor is
then handed no shift and the layout returns the unshifted character. A
cascadia pane is correct on the same keystrokes because its text comes
from CharacterReceived, which Windows generates from the message stream.

This is not KD-08 again. That one reported the wrong *consumed*
modifiers and only kitty-protocol clients noticed; this one loses shift
before any of that, in every application.

GhosttyModifiersFor tracks the modifier keys from their own key events -
which are ordered, so the shift press is delivered before the key it
modifies - and returns the snapshot unioned with what it has seen held.
The union is in that direction because a modifier held before this pane
had focus is in the snapshot and was never seen as an event. The
opposite risk, a modifier believed held that is not, is bounded by
clearing the tracked set on both GotFocus and LostFocus, since a release
that happens while another window owns the keyboard never arrives here.

Caps Lock is not tracked: it is a state rather than a hold, is reported
as locked, and is not subject to the race.

The corrected modifiers also feed ev.mods, the KD-08 consumed-modifier
computation and GhosttyExitKeyFor, so the text and the encoding cannot
disagree about what was held.

Measured with harness/rawin, which names the bytes the child received.
Before, typed from Android: `;` `/` `a`. The mechanism reproduced on both
builds by posting the key messages to the XAML input site, so shift
exists as an event and never as state: 0.2.10 gives `;` `;` `/` `a`,
this gives `;` `:` `?` `A`.

unitControl 73/73, including two new pure tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
